            <li><a href="#conclusion">Conclusion</a></li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>
          Picture two nights out. On the first, you and a friend spend ten
          minutes weighing two restaurants, land on one, and the food is
          mediocre. On the second, you flip a coin, it sends you to the same
          mediocre meal. The food is identical. Your reaction usually isn't. The
          chosen bad meal tends to sit with you — you start replaying the
          decision, wondering what you missed. The random bad meal is easier to
          laugh off.
        </p>
        <p>
          That gap isn't irrational. It's a predictable feature of how people
          process responsibility, fairness, and regret — and it's the same
          psychology that explains why tools like a
          <a href="/blogs/spin-the-wheel-tool" class="ilink"
            >spin the wheel tool</a
          >
          show up everywhere from classrooms to team stand-ups to family
          dinners. Not because randomness is magic, but because it sidesteps a
          set of very specific mental costs that deliberate choosing carries
          with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          This piece walks through what's actually happening — psychologically
          and neurologically — when a decision gets handed to chance, and where
          that handoff genuinely helps versus where it doesn't.
        </p>

        <figure class="ai-image">
          <img
            src="/eng-yes-no-wheel.webp"
