        <h2 id="regret">
          6. The Regret Gap Between Chosen and Random Outcomes
        </h2>
        <p>
          One of the steadier findings in decision research is that people feel
          worse about a bad outcome that followed their own active choice than
          about an equally bad outcome that came from doing nothing or from a
          process outside their control. Psychologists call this an action
          effect: the act of choosing amplifies regret, even when the physical
          result is identical either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          Apply that to everyday selection — a picked restaurant disappoints, an
          assigned project turns out tedious, a chosen teammate underdelivers —
          and random selection consistently softens the blow. There's no
          decision-maker left to blame, including yourself. That's genuinely
          useful for anyone who has to make repeated calls that will sometimes
          go wrong: managers, teachers, parents.
        </p>

        <div class="box box-success">
          <div class="box-label">✅ Why this matters for leaders</div>
          <p>
            Beyond fewer complaints, people who hand routine assignment
            decisions to a random tool often report less personal stress about
            them. Knowing a fair process picked the outcome — not your own
            judgment — takes the edge off the anticipatory worry that makes
            small management decisions feel bigger than they should.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="satisfaction">
          7. Why We're Often Happier With What We Didn't Pick
        </h2>
        <p>
          Here's the part that seems backwards: people are frequently more
          satisfied with outcomes they didn't choose than with ones they
          agonised over. More control should, in theory, mean more satisfaction.
          In practice, it often doesn't.
        </p>
        <h3>Too many good options</h3>
        <p>
          The more options on the table and the longer you deliberate, the
          higher your expectations climb — and the bigger the letdown when
          reality doesn't match them. A random pick carries none of that
          built-up expectation. You get what you get, with no "it should have
          been better" yardstick to measure it against.
        </p>
        <h3>Making peace with the result</h3>
        <p>
          People are also good at finding reasons a random outcome is actually
          fine, or even interesting — a habit psychologists call post-decisional
          rationalisation. Students randomly assigned an unfamiliar topic for a
          project often end up more engaged with it than students who picked
          something safe and familiar, partly because the randomness itself
          sparks curiosity.
        </p>
        <h3>A decision that actually closes</h3>
        <p>
          Open decisions quietly use up mental bandwidth even while you're doing
          something else — the itch of an unresolved choice sitting in the back
          of your mind. A spin closes that loop instantly. Once it lands,
          there's no re-litigating it. That finality is a big part of why the
          moment feels satisfying, independent of what the wheel actually landed
          on.
        </p>

