        <h2 id="faq">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Is it ever rational to let a random tool make an important
            decision?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            For a specific category, yes. When the options are genuinely close
            in value, when you're too fatigued to judge well, or when the social
            cost of appearing to choose outweighs the actual stakes, randomness
            is often the sensible move. High-stakes, one-off, asymmetric
            decisions are the clear exception.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Why do children accept a wheel's result more easily than a parent's
            decision?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Children have a strong, early-developing sense of procedural
            fairness. A parent's call can always be argued with — favouritism,
            mood, timing. A wheel leaves nothing to argue with, so kids tend to
            accept the result with far less pushback, provided they watched it
            happen.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Does relying on random tools undermine good leadership?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Not for the decisions they're suited to. Leadership means handling
            the choices that genuinely need judgment — not personally
            adjudicating every minor pick. Using a wheel for routine, low-stakes
            assignments frees that judgment up for where it actually matters.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            What is choice paralysis, and how does randomness help?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Choice paralysis is the stuck feeling that shows up when options
            seem equally good, or the stakes feel higher than they are. It's
            driven by fear of choosing wrong. Handing the decision to chance
            removes the choosing itself — and often, your reaction to the result
            (relief or a flicker of disappointment) tells you what you actually
            wanted.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            When is random selection a bad idea?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            When outcomes carry real, uneven consequences — medical, legal,
            financial calls — or when expertise should clearly decide the
            result. It's also wrong for decisions where one option is genuinely,
            demonstrably better; in those cases the answer is worth finding
            through thought, not chance.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Why use a spinning wheel instead of a plain random number?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            The spin itself creates shared anticipation and makes the process
            visible to everyone watching — a number appearing on screen gives a
            result but not an experience. In group settings, that visible, felt
            fairness is often what earns buy-in. More on the mechanics in
            <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random" class="ilink"
              >is a spin wheel truly random</a
            >.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="conclusion">10. Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
          The psychology here isn't an argument for laziness — it's a case for
          being honest about which decisions actually need your judgment and
          which are just quietly draining it. Deliberate choice has real costs:
          cognitive, social, emotional. Random selection sidesteps most of them
          at once. It cuts decision fatigue, maximises how fair a process feels,
          removes the usual human biases, holds attention through genuine
          uncertainty, softens regret, and closes the loop in a way that
          drawn-out deliberation rarely manages.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that means every decision belongs to a wheel. It means the
          low-stakes, frequent, roughly-equal ones probably do — and letting go
          of those isn't a surrender, it's just good use of a limited resource.
        </p>
