              Random outcomes are accepted with less regret than chosen ones,
              even when the result is identical.
            </li>
            <li>
              Decision fatigue is cumulative — offloading small choices protects
              capacity for the ones that count.
            </li>
            <li>
              A process feeling fair matters more for satisfaction than the
              outcome itself being good.
            </li>
            <li>
              Human selection carries built-in biases that a genuine random
              process simply doesn't have.
            </li>
            <li>
              Shared uncertainty during a live spin creates real, collective
              attention.
            </li>
            <li>
              A visible spin adds the felt experience of fairness — not just the
              fact of it.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>


